# Rust conventions (server)

## `mod.rs` is a glossary

A `mod.rs` holds a one-line `//!` header, `mod` declarations and curated
`pub use` re-exports. Nothing else: no handlers, no routers, no types, no
helpers. Logic goes into a named sibling file (`router.rs`, `list.rs`, …).

```rust
// ✅ server/src/storage/mod.rs
//! Files under the `STORAGE` root. Knows nothing about HTTP.

mod error;
mod list;
mod path;

pub use error::StorageError;
pub use list::{Entry, list_dir};
pub use path::resolve;
```

- Submodules are private `mod`; the `pub use` list is the public surface.
  No `pub mod`, no `pub use foo::*`.
- Visibility is as narrow as callers allow (`pub(super)` before `pub`). Don't
  widen "in case".
- A file that needs `// --- section ---` dividers wants splitting instead.

## Layout

- `src/<domain>/` — a domain (`storage/`, later `auth/`, …). Pure logic and
  I/O, no axum/HTTP types. Domain errors are a `thiserror` enum in
  `<domain>/error.rs`.
- `src/routes/<area>/` — thin HTTP handlers: extract, call the domain, map
  the result to a response DTO. One file per endpoint (`ls.rs`), plus
  `router.rs` composing them.
- Top-level files are app-wide plumbing: `config.rs`, `state.rs`,
  `error.rs`, `telemetry.rs`, `main.rs`. When one grows (middlewares, …),
  turn it into a folder with the same glossary `mod.rs`.

## Errors

- Handlers return `Result<_, AppError>`. Each domain error gets an
  `impl From<DomainError> for AppError` in `error.rs`, so handlers just use
  `?`.
- Error messages reach the client: never put absolute server paths or
  other internals in them. `AppError::Internal` is logged and replaced with
  a generic message.
- `anyhow` is for `main`/startup only, not for domain code.

## Config

- Config is parsed once in `main` and shared as `Arc<Config>` in `AppState`.
  Never call `std::env::var` elsewhere.

## Wire format

- Field names are snake_case on the wire, same as Rust. No
  `#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase")]` — the client converts at its API
  boundary.
- Domain entities (`storage::Entry`, …) derive `Serialize` and are returned
  as is. Add a separate response DTO next to the handler only once the wire
  shape has to differ (hidden fields, different units).

## User-supplied paths

Reject, don't normalize: `.`, `..`, empty segments in the middle, or anything
escaping the root is a `400`. Symlinks are never followed: hidden from
listings, and a `404` anywhere on a requested path. Every filesystem access
goes through `storage::resolve`.

## Checks

`cargo fmt`, `npm run clippy` (`-D warnings`), and
`cargo test --manifest-path server/Cargo.toml` must pass.
